keywords: ["cellular automata", "probabilistic cellular automata", "noise", "ergodicity", "undecidability", "halting problem", "Gibbs measures", "spontaneous magnetization", "noise diagram"]
abstract: "We construct one finite-range cellular automaton on the line with computable disjoint rational windows of uniform replacement noise. Throughout the window indexed by a program, the perturbed automaton is uniformly exponentially ergodic from every initial configuration if the program halts. If it runs forever, a one-site clock observable fails to converge from a specified initial law. The same dichotomy holds at one fixed positive rational noise rate for a computable family of radius-one rules. On the square lattice, the single-rule construction gives one translation-invariant finite-range interaction whose anchored terms have energies zero and one, with a fixed-point-free symmetry of its alphabet. In each halting window of inverse temperature its Dobrushin-Lanford-Ruelle specification has a unique Gibbs measure, with exponential weak spatial mixing. In each nonhalting window it has two translation-invariant Gibbs measures exchanged by the symmetry, under which an odd one-site observable has expectation at least 1/4 and at most -1/4, respectively. The construction uses a hierarchy of fault-tolerant simulations and autonomous spatial signals that encode the noise window."
